<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-types-of-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Types of Functions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Identify functions and their properties
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To help learners connect the idea of a function to something familiar before introducing formal definitions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Pose the activity below to learners without giving away the answer. Allow learners to discuss in pairs, then bring the class together to share ideas. Use their responses to introduce the formal definition of a function.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->
    <activity>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A school assigns each learner a unique admission number. Whenever the admission number is entered into the school system, the details of that learner are displayed.
            </p>

            <p>
                Discuss the following questions with a partner.
            </p>

            <ol marker="1.">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Can one admission number belong to two different learners at the same time? Explain your answer.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Can two different admission numbers belong to the same learner? Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In this situation, the admission number is the <em>input</em> and the learner identified by that number is the <em>output</em>. What rule must the school system follow for the records to remain accurate?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                Based on your discussion, write a sentence describing what you think a function is.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    20 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To build on what learners noticed in the activity and connect it to the formal types of functions they will work with throughout the course.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> After the vending machine activity, draw out the key idea that each input gives exactly one output. Tell learners that this is the defining property of a function, and that the rest of the lesson shows them the different shapes and forms this rule can take.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Insight Block:</alert> Walk learners through the four types of functions in the insight block. For each type, ask learners to identify the highest power of <m>x</m> before you label it. This keeps them active rather than passive.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>One-to-One Functions:</alert> Emphasise that a function can have two inputs sharing the same output (like two vending buttons giving the same snack) and still be a valid function. A one-to-one function simply adds the extra condition that no two inputs share the same output. Use the horizontal line test as a quick visual check.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
          <paragraphs>
            <title>Common Types of Functions</title>
            <p>
                A <term>function</term> is a rule where every input gives exactly one output. The four types below are the ones you will meet most often.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="1.">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <term>Linear Functions</term> have the form <m>f(x) = mx + c</m>. Their graphs are straight lines, and the slope <m>m</m> tells you the constant rate of change.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <term>Quadratic Functions</term> have the form <m>f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m>. Their graphs are U-shaped curves called <term>parabolas</term>, with a turning point called the <term>vertex</term> that marks the highest or lowest value of the function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <term>Polynomial Functions</term> include linear and quadratic functions as special cases, and extend to higher powers such as <m>x^3</m> (cubic) and <m>x^4</m> (quartic). All exponents are whole numbers greater than or equal to zero, and the graphs are smooth continuous curves that may bend more than once.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <term>Trigonometric Functions</term> include <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>, <m>f(x) = \cos(x)</m>, and <m>f(x) = \tan(x)</m>. These functions produce wave-like graphs that repeat the same pattern over and over at regular intervals.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <figure xml:id="fig-types-of-functions">
            <caption>Graphs illustrating Linear, Quadratic, and Trigonometric functions.</caption>
            <image xml:id="tikz-types-of-functions" width="100%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Three graphs side-by-side. The first is a straight line, the second is a parabola opening upwards, and the third is a repeating sine wave.
                </shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]

                        % ================= LINEAR GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(-6,0)}]
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Graph
                            \draw[blue, thick, domain=-2:2] plot (\x, {1.5*\x + 1});
                            
                            % Labels
                            \node at (0,-3) {\textbf{Linear Function}};
                            \node at (0,-3.5) {$f(x) = mx + c$};
                        \end{scope}

                        % ================= QUADRATIC GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(0,0)}]
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Graph
                            \draw[red, thick, domain=-2:2, samples=100] plot (\x, {\x*\x - 1});
                            
                            % Vertex dot
                            \filldraw[black] (0,-1) circle (2pt);
                            \node at (0.5, -1) [below right] {\small Vertex};
                            
                            % Labels
                            \node at (0,-3) {\textbf{Quadratic Function}};
                            \node at (0,-3.5) {$f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c$};
                        \end{scope}

                        % ================= TRIGONOMETRIC GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(6,0)}]
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Graph
                            \draw[purple, thick, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {1.5*sin(\x*100)});
                            
                            % Labels
                            \node at (0,-3) {\textbf{Trigonometric Function}};
                            \node at (0,-3.5) {$f(x) = \sin(x)$};
                        \end{scope}

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </insight>

    <exploration>
    <title>Horizontal Line Test Explorer</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-function-types-line-test" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/function-types-line-test.js">
        <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-function-types-line-test" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            With <c>Linear</c> selected, drag the <m>y</m>-slider up and down. Does the number of orange intersection points ever change? What does this tell you about linear functions and the one-to-one property?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Switch to <c>Quadratic</c> and drag the slider so the horizontal line passes above the vertex. How many intersection points appear? Compare this to Example 3 in this section, where <m>h(x) = x^2</m> was shown to fail the horizontal line test.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Switch to <c>Polynomial</c> (the cubic) and find a height where the line crosses the curve three times. Then find a height where it only crosses once. Is a cubic function always one-to-one, sometimes one-to-one, or never one-to-one?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Switch to <c>Trigonometric</c> and slide through several heights. Explain why a wave-like graph is especially unlikely to be one-to-one over its full domain, connecting your answer to the Advanced Learner challenge about restricting the domain of trigonometric functions.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Based on everything you tested, write your own definition of a one-to-one function in your own words, without looking back at the text.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Identify the type of function for <m>f(x) = -3x + 7</m> and state its rate of change.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This function is written in the form <m>f(x) = mx + c</m>. Therefore, it is a <alert>linear function</alert>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The graph of this function is a straight line. Its rate of change is constant and is represented by the slope <m>m</m>. In this equation, the slope is <m>-3</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the function <m>g(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + 5</m>. Identify the type of function and determine whether its vertex is a maximum or a minimum.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Because the highest exponent of the variable <m>x</m> is 2, this is a <alert>quadratic function</alert>. Its graph is a parabola.
            </p>
            <p>
                To determine if the vertex is a maximum or minimum, we look at the leading coefficient, <m>a</m>. Here, <m>a = 2</m>. Since <m>a</m> is positive (<m>a &gt; 0</m>), the parabola opens upwards. 
            </p>
            <p>
                Because the parabola opens upwards, its turning point (vertex) represents the lowest point on the graph. Therefore, the vertex is a <alert>minimum</alert>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine if the quadratic function <m>h(x) = x^2</m> is a one-to-one function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For a function to be one-to-one, every <m>y</m>-value must be paired with exactly one <m>x</m>-value. Let's test a couple of inputs:
            </p>
            <p>
                If <m>x = 2</m>, <m>h(2) = 2^2 = 4</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                If <m>x = -2</m>, <m>h(-2) = (-2)^2 = 4</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Because two different inputs (<m>2</m> and <m>-2</m>) yield the exact same output (<m>4</m>), the function fails the horizontal line test. Therefore, <m>h(x) = x^2</m> is <alert>not a one-to-one function</alert>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Classify the function <m>p(x) = 4x^5 - 3x^3 + x - 9</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This function consists of multiple terms with variables raised to non-negative integer powers. Because the highest power is 5 (which is greater than 2), it is a higher-degree function.
            </p>
            <p>
                Specifically, this is a <alert>polynomial function</alert> of degree 5.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercise-types-of-functions">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Categorize each of the following equations as a Linear Function, Quadratic Function, Polynomial Function (higher degree), Trigonometric Function, or Not a Function (a Relation).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x) = 5x - 12</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>g(x) = -x^2 + 4x + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>h(x) = \cos(x) + 2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>p(x) = 7x^4 - 2x^2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x^2 + y^2 = 25</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>q(x) = \frac{1}{2}x</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>r(x) = \tan(x)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>s(x) = x^3 - 8</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="1." cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Linear Function
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Quadratic Function
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Trigonometric Function
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Polynomial Function (Degree 4)
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Not a Function (Relation / Circle)
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Linear Function
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Trigonometric Function
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Polynomial Function (Cubic)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine whether each of the following descriptions represents a <alert>function</alert> or a <alert>relation that is not a function</alert>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>A graph of a circle with a radius of 3 centered at the origin.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>A mapping where the input is a student's ID number and the output is the student's legal first name.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>A set of coordinate pairs: <m>\{(1, 2), (2, 4), (1, 5), (3, 6)\}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>Relation (fails the vertical line test)</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Function (each unique ID maps to exactly one name)</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relation (the input 1 maps to multiple outputs: 2 and 5)</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which of the following functions are <alert>one-to-one</alert>? Explain your reasoning using the horizontal line test or algebraic properties.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>f(x) = 3x - 2</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>g(x) = |x|</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>h(x) = x^3</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>One-to-one. It is a straight, slanted line with a constant slope, so it passes the horizontal line test.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Not one-to-one. It forms a V-shape, meaning a positive and a negative input (e.g., <m>x = 2</m> and <m>x = -2</m>) share the exact same output (<m>2</m>).</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>One-to-one. The cubic curve strictly increases across its entire domain, passing the horizontal line test.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A company calculates its daily revenue, <m>R</m>, based on the number of items sold, <m>x</m>, using the equation <m>R(x) = -2x^2 + 100x</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>What type of function is <m>R(x)</m>?</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Does the graph of this function have a maximum or a minimum turning point? Explain why.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>Quadratic Function</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Maximum. The leading coefficient (<m>a = -2</m>) is a negative number, meaning the parabola opens downwards, making its vertex the highest point on the graph.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Focus only on linear and quadratic functions in the first session. Use the vending machine analogy throughout and provide a printed reference card showing each function type, its form, and a simple sketch of its graph. For exercises, start with parts (a), (b), and (f) of Exercise 1 only, and allow learners to use graphing tools to verify their answers visually.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        After completing the exercises, ask advanced learners to find a real-life situation that could be modelled by each of the four function types and write one or two sentences explaining their choice. They can also explore whether trigonometric functions are one-to-one on their full domain and what happens when you restrict the domain.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>


